WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.569
Memory operations in C operate on bytes.

2
00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:11.433
That sounds straightforward, but many bugs start when code confuses byte counts, character counts, element counts, and allocated capacity.

3
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:16.893
A structure array with ten elements is not ten bytes unless each element is one byte.

4
00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:22.610
A string length does not include terminator space unless the caller adds it deliberately.

5
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:32.373
Before calling a memory operation, the code should know the source pointer, destination pointer, number of bytes to move, and valid size of each region.

6
00:00:32.373 --> 00:00:41.815
If the count was computed from element size and element count, that calculation should be checked for overflow before allocation or copying occurs.

7
00:00:41.815 --> 00:00:43.549
Overlap assumptions matter.

8
00:00:43.549 --> 00:00:48.046
Some operations require source and destination regions not to overlap.

9
00:00:48.046 --> 00:00:53.377
When overlap is possible, the code should use the operation designed for that case.

10
00:00:53.377 --> 00:01:02.113
Reviewers should not rely on current data layout by accident; they should ask whether the contract forbids overlap or handles it safely.

11
00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:06.031
Allocation math should distinguish capacity from used length.

12
00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:11.041
A buffer may allocate space for many elements while only some are initialized.

13
00:01:11.041 --> 00:01:18.620
A helper that receives both capacity and used length can make it harder to read or write outside the meaningful range.

14
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:24.080
That same helper can check whether appending or copying would exceed available space.

15
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.649
Clearing memory also deserves attention.

16
00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:37.055
When buffers contain sensitive values, the project should use a reliable library or platform-supported clearing mechanism where compiler optimization is relevant.

17
00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:47.203
For ordinary buffers, cleanup still needs to match ownership and lifetime rules so that callers do not free the same allocation twice or leak it indefinitely.

18
00:01:47.203 --> 00:01:54.333
A clear buffer contract defines pointer, capacity, used length, element count, ownership, and failure behavior.

19
00:01:54.333 --> 00:02:05.124
Small helper functions can centralize these checks so that reviewers see the same pattern repeatedly instead of revalidating fragile size arithmetic at every call site.
